1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:08,672
Agent Skills in 60s: you stop loading your 1 big prompt before each task; skills cost 100 tokens until you need them, but how?

2
00:00:09,172 --> 00:00:10,395
Here's how, in a kitchen.

3
00:00:10,695 --> 00:00:14,390
Every rule you wrote sits in one giant binder: your big prompt.

4
00:00:14,690 --> 00:00:17,549
Your cook rereads all of it, even to fry one egg.

5
00:00:18,049 --> 00:00:19,980
Agent Skills are a recipe box.

6
00:00:20,280 --> 00:00:23,143
Every card has a tab: its name, and when to use it.

7
00:00:23,443 --> 00:00:25,006
Your cook reads only the tabs.

8
00:00:25,306 --> 00:00:27,974
Soup is ordered: he pulls only the soup card.

9
00:00:28,474 --> 00:00:31,198
A skill is a folder with a SKILL.md file.

10
00:00:31,498 --> 00:00:35,300
At startup, your agent loads only each skill's name and description:

11
00:00:35,360 --> 00:00:36,710
about 100 tokens.

12
00:00:37,010 --> 00:00:39,803
When a task matches, it reads the full instructions.

13
00:00:40,103 --> 00:00:42,113
Extra files load only if needed.

14
00:00:42,613 --> 00:00:46,155
That's the how: only the tab loads, until a task needs the card.

15
00:00:46,455 --> 00:00:49,671
So your agent keeps many skills on hand, and room to work.

16
00:00:50,171 --> 00:00:52,145
The lesson is as old as the kitchen.

17
00:00:52,445 --> 00:00:53,981
Don't tell your agent everything.

18
00:00:54,281 --> 00:00:55,169
Tell it where to look.

19
00:00:55,669 --> 00:00:57,071
Subscribe for the next one.
